---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import MoreInCategory from "../components/MoreInCategory.astro";
import AuthorBio from "../components/AuthorBio.astro";
import { authorSchema } from "../data/authors";
import AuthorAvatar from "../components/AuthorAvatar.astro";
import TrustBadge from "../components/TrustBadge.astro";
import SocialProof from "../components/SocialProof.astro";
import { pageDates } from "../data/dates";

const moreInCategory = [
    { title: "Best Electric Pressure Washers", url: "/best-electric-pressure-washer/" },
    { title: "What PSI Do I Need?", url: "/what-psi-pressure-washer-do-i-need/" },
    { title: "Best Surface Cleaners", url: "/best-surface-cleaner-for-pressure-washer/" },
];
const dates = pageDates(Astro.url.pathname, { published: "2026-09-06", modified: "2026-09-06" });
const title = "Pressure Washer Nozzle Colors Explained";
const description =
  "Red, yellow, green, white and black. What each angle does, which surfaces each belongs on, and why the black tip is the only one that draws detergent.";

const schema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@graph": [
    {
      "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
      itemListElement: [
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 1,
          name: "Home",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 2,
          name: "Guides",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/best-of/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 3,
          name: "Nozzle Colors Explained",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/pressure-washer-nozzle-colors-explained/",
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "What do pressure washer nozzle colors mean?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "They follow an industry convention for spray angle. Red is 0 degrees, yellow 15, green 25, white 40, and black is a wide low-pressure soap tip. Wider angles spread the same output over more area, so they hit each square inch less hard and cover more ground per pass.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Which pressure washer nozzle should I use most?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "The white 40 degree tip, for most household work. It suits vehicles, vinyl and painted siding, patio furniture, and rinsing anything after a cleaner has done the chemical work. People under-use it because it feels gentle, but gentle plus a good detergent cleans better than force.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "What is the red 0 degree nozzle for?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Narrow professional tasks like cutting a stubborn deposit off a machine part or clearing a blocked line. On household surfaces it removes material rather than dirt, and because it covers essentially no area it cannot clean efficiently anyway. Most owners never find a legitimate use for it.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Why is no soap coming out of my pressure washer?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Almost always because a high-pressure tip is fitted. The detergent siphon only engages at low pressure, so it stops drawing entirely with any tip other than the black one. Fit the black nozzle, and check the siphon tube and filter are in the detergent and not kinked.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "What nozzle should I use on a wood deck?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "The green 25 degree tip at 1,200 to 1,800 PSI, keeping the wand moving constantly and working with the grain. Pausing is what raises the grain and leaves a furred, splintery surface that only sanding will fix. Applying a deck cleaner first lets you use less pressure throughout.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Are turbo nozzles safe?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "On sound concrete, yes, and they are very effective there. Everywhere else, no. A turbo tip spins a 0 degree jet in a cone, so it delivers red-tip force across a wider path and will fur wood and mark siding exactly as a red tip would, only across more area.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Are adjustable nozzles as good as separate tips?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "They are more convenient and usually a little less precise. A variable tip that twists between angles saves swapping nozzles mid-job, which matters on a large surface, but fixed tips generally give a cleaner, more consistent pattern at each angle.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Do all pressure washers use the same nozzles?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Most use a standard quarter-inch quick-connect, so tips are interchangeable across brands. Some manufacturers, including certain premium ones, use a proprietary connection instead, which limits you to their accessories permanently. Check the fitting before buying any attachment.",
          },
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "Article",
      headline: title,
      description: description,
      author: authorSchema(),
      datePublished: dates.published,
      dateModified: dates.modified,
      publisher: { "@type": "Organization", name: "The Honest Reviewers" },
    },
  ],
};
---

<Layout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  ogType="article"
  article={{
    publishedTime: dates.published,
    modifiedTime: dates.modified,
    author: "Alex Rivers",
    section: "Tools & Equipment",
    tags: [
      "pressure washer nozzle colors",
      "pressure washer tips explained",
      "what nozzle for pressure washing",
      "tools & equipment",
    ],
  }}
  jsonLd={schema}
  stickyCta={{
    text: "See Our Top Electric Pressure Washers",
    link: "/best-electric-pressure-washer/",
  }}
>
  <article class="relative overflow-hidden">
    <!-- Hero -->
    <div class="bg-gray-900 py-16 lg:py-24 relative overflow-hidden">
      <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 relative z-10 text-center">
        <div class="flex justify-center mb-6">
          <TrustBadge />
        </div>
        <div class="flex justify-center gap-3 mb-6">
          <span class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-3 py-1.5 rounded-full bg-[#FF4500]/20 text-[#FF4500] text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest border border-[#FF4500]/30">
            Reference
          </span>
          <span class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-3 py-1.5 rounded-full bg-green-500/10 text-green-400 text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-widest border border-green-500/20">
            Field Tested
          </span>
        </div>
        <h1 class="text-4xl lg:text-6xl font-black text-white leading-tight mb-8">
          Pressure Washer <span class="text-[#38BDF8]">Nozzle Colors</span>
        </h1>
        <p class="text-xl text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-8 max-w-3xl mx-auto">
          The tip decides what actually reaches the surface. A red nozzle on a modest machine hits harder at a point than a white nozzle on a powerful one, which is why nozzle discipline prevents more damage than buying less pressure does.
        </p>
        <div class="flex flex-col sm:flex-row gap-4 justify-center">
          <a
            href="#the-colors"
            class="px-8 py-4 bg-[#FF4500] hover:bg-[#E63E00] text-white font-black rounded-xl shadow-lg transition-all no-underline"
          >
            See the Color Chart
          </a>
          <a
            href="#faq"
            class="px-8 py-4 bg-white/10 hover:bg-white/20 text-white font-bold rounded-xl border border-white/10 no-underline"
          >
            Jump to the FAQ
          </a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Author & Meta -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pt-12">
      <div class="flex items-center gap-6 border-b border-gray-100 pb-8">
        <div class="flex items-center gap-3">
          <AuthorAvatar size={48} />
          <div>
            <p class="text-sm font-bold text-gray-900">Alex Rivers</p>
            <p class="text-xs text-gray-500 uppercase tracking-widest">Home Improvement Editor</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="h-10 w-px bg-gray-200 hidden sm:block"></div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block">
          <p class="text-sm font-bold text-gray-900">Last Updated</p>
          <p class="text-xs text-gray-500">{dates.display}</p>
        </div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block ml-auto">
          <SocialProof users="21.4k" />
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Table of Contents -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-10">
      <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8">
        <h2 class="text-lg font-black text-gray-900 mb-4 flex items-center gap-2">
          In This Guide
        </h2>
        <nav class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-2">
          <a href="#the-colors" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">1. The Five Colors</a>
          <a href="#red" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">2. Red, 0 Degrees</a>
          <a href="#yellow-green" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">3. Yellow and Green</a>
          <a href="#white" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">4. White, 40 Degrees</a>
          <a href="#black" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">5. Black, Soap</a>
          <a href="#other-tips" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">6. Other Tips</a>
          <a href="#quick-reference" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">7. Quick Reference</a>
          <a href="#common-mistakes" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">8. Common Mistakes to Avoid</a>
          <a href="#faq" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">9. Frequently Asked Questions</a>
        </nav>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Body -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          Pressure washer tips are colour-coded to an industry convention, so a green nozzle means the same thing on any machine. Learning the five colours is the single highest-return ten minutes in this entire category.
        </p>

        <div id="the-colors" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. The Five Colors
          </h2>

          <p>
            Each tip takes the machine's full output and spreads it across a different angle. Wider spread means less force per square inch at the surface, and more area covered per pass.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Color</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Angle</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Force at the Surface</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Use For</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Red</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">0&deg;</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Maximum, pencil-thin jet</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Almost nothing; damages most surfaces</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Yellow</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">15&deg;</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">High, narrow path</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Aggressive work on hard surfaces</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Green</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">25&deg;</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Moderate, useful path</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">General purpose, decks, concrete</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">White</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">40&deg;</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Gentle, wide path</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Siding, vehicles, delicate materials</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Black</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Wide, low pressure</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Lowest</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">The only tip that draws detergent</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            The pattern is consistent: as the angle widens, force concentrates less and coverage increases. That trade is the entire decision. You want the widest nozzle that still does the job, because it covers more ground per pass and leaves the least chance of damaging what you are cleaning.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">The Habit Worth Building</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Start wide, start far back, step down one angle at a time.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Begin with the widest tip you have, standing roughly ten feet from the surface, and close the distance gradually while watching what the spray does. If the widest tip at close range still is not shifting the dirt, step down one nozzle angle, not three. This prevents almost every case of etched siding, furred wood and stripped paint, because you find the surface limit before you cross it.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="red" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Red: The One to Leave in the Box
          </h2>

          <p>
            The red tip concentrates the machine's entire output into a jet a few millimetres across. It does not spread at all, which means every bit of the pressure and flow arrives at one tiny point.
          </p>

          <p>
            It will cut into wood, strip paint, etch concrete, lacerate skin and destroy almost anything it touches for more than a second. Because it covers essentially no area, it is also useless for actual cleaning, since you would be working a slab one pencil-width at a time.
          </p>

          <ul class="space-y-2 my-6">
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span>It is included because there are narrow professional uses: cutting a stubborn deposit off a machine part, clearing a blocked drain line, marking a line in dirt.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span>On any household surface it removes material rather than dirt, and the damage is instant and permanent.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span>It is the tip responsible for most pressure washer injuries, because it concentrates force in the same way a blade does.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span>A red tip on a modest machine delivers more force at a point than a white tip on a far more powerful one.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span>If you have never found a use for it, that is the correct outcome rather than a gap in your technique.</span>
            </li>
          </ul>

          <p>
            The practical advice from everyone who has used these machines seriously is the same: take the red tip out of the holder and put it somewhere separate, so it cannot be grabbed by accident when you are working quickly and reaching for green.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="yellow-green" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Yellow and Green: The Working Tips
          </h2>

          <p>
            These two do most of the genuine cleaning on hard surfaces, and choosing between them is usually the only real nozzle decision you make.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Yellow, 15 Degrees</h3>

          <p>
            Aggressive, with a narrow path. It belongs on hard, sound surfaces where you need to lift something stubborn: paint preparation, heavy build-up on concrete, stripping a coating deliberately. It is too aggressive for wood, siding, vehicles and anything with a coating you want to keep.
          </p>

          <p>
            Treat yellow as a spot tool rather than a general one. Working a whole driveway with it is slow, produces obvious striping, and risks etching the surface. Use it on the patch that green will not shift, then go back to green.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Green, 25 Degrees</h3>

          <p>
            The general-purpose tip and the one most jobs should default to on hard surfaces. It has enough force to lift embedded dirt from concrete and enough spread to cover ground at a reasonable rate. It is the right choice for decks, for concrete when you are using a wand rather than a surface cleaner, and for the inside of wheel arches.
          </p>

          <p>
            On wood decking, green is the correct tip and technique carries the rest: keep the wand moving constantly, hold a consistent distance, and work with the grain. Pausing is what raises the grain and leaves the furred surface that only sanding fixes.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="white" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. White: The One You Should Use Most
          </h2>

          <p>
            The 40 degree white tip is the safest and most useful nozzle in the set for household work, and it is the one most people under-use because it feels too gentle to be doing anything.
          </p>

          <p>
            It spreads the output across a wide fan, which means low force per square inch and a lot of area covered per pass. That is exactly right for the surfaces most of us actually clean: vinyl siding, painted wood, vehicles, patio furniture, and rinsing anything after a cleaner has done the chemical work.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Job</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Right Tip</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Why</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Vehicle paint and glass</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">White</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Only safe choice on clear coat</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Vinyl siding</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">White, or green if needed</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Wide coverage, low risk</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Painted wood siding</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">White</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Paint lifts under anything narrower</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Patio furniture</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">White</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Plastic and fabric mark easily</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Rinsing after detergent</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">White</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Chemistry did the work; you are just rinsing</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Soffits and fascia</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">White</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Delicate, and often overhead</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Windows and frames</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">White, at distance</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Seals and glazing are vulnerable</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            The reason white feels weak is that people compare it against the red tip's ability to blast a mark off concrete. But the actual measure is whether a surface comes clean, and once a proper cleaner has dwelled on it, the white tip rinses off dirt that a narrow tip would have had to fight.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="black" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Black: The One Everyone Misunderstands
          </h2>

          <p>
            The black tip is not a cleaning nozzle. It is a low-pressure tip whose entire purpose is to let the machine draw detergent, and it is the single most misunderstood item in the box.
          </p>

          <p>
            The detergent siphon on a pressure washer only engages at low pressure. Fitting a high-pressure tip raises the pressure at the nozzle, the siphon stops drawing, and no soap comes through. That is the most common reason people conclude their detergent system is broken when it is working exactly as designed.
          </p>

          <ul class="space-y-2 my-6">
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span>Fit the black tip before you expect any detergent to appear. Nothing else in the set will draw it.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span>Apply detergent bottom to top on vertical surfaces, so it cannot run down dry areas and streak.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span>Let it dwell for several minutes without drying, which is where the actual cleaning happens.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span>Switch to a rinsing tip, usually white or green, and rinse from the top down.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span>Flush clean water through the system afterwards so detergent does not sit in the machine.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span>Losing the black tip is remarkably common, and it is the one you cannot substitute for.</span>
            </li>
          </ul>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">Keep Track of This One</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">The black nozzle is the one you cannot improvise.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Every other tip has a near neighbour that will do at a pinch: green instead of yellow, white instead of green. Lose the black tip and the detergent system is simply unavailable, because no high-pressure tip will let the siphon draw. Machines with on-board nozzle storage largely solve this; if yours does not, a small box on a shelf does.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="other-tips" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Adjustable, Turbo and Specialist Tips
          </h2>

          <p>
            Beyond the five colours there are a few designs worth knowing about, one of which is genuinely useful and one of which is frequently misused.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Type</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">What It Does</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Worth It?</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Adjustable / variable</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Twists between spray angles in one tip</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Convenient; usually less precise than fixed tips</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Turbo / rotary</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Spins a 0&deg; jet in a cone</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Effective on concrete; dangerous elsewhere</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Soap / foam cannon</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Mixes thick foam for dwell</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Excellent for vehicles</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Surface cleaner</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Shrouded spinning disc</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">The best accessory for flat concrete</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Extension wand</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Reaches height from the ground</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Removes ladder work entirely</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Gutter attachment</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Curved end for clearing gutters</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Useful, saves ladder time</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Undercarriage lance</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Sprays upward under a vehicle</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Genuinely useful in salted climates</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Turbo nozzles deserve a specific warning. They spin a 0 degree jet in a circular pattern, which gives red-tip cleaning force across a green-tip path, and on sound concrete they work extremely well. On wood, siding, paint or anything soft they will do exactly what a red tip does, only across a wider area, so treat them as a concrete-only accessory.
          </p>

          <p>
            Two fitting details are worth checking before buying anything. Most machines use a standard quick-connect, but some brands, notably certain premium ones, use a proprietary connection that limits you to their accessories forever. And check the minimum flow a surface cleaner or foam cannon needs, since a low-GPM machine may not run one properly.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="quick-reference" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. A Quick Reference by Surface
          </h2>

          <p>
            The chart to keep in the garage. Match the surface, then use the pressure column as a sanity check on the machine setting.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Surface</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Nozzle</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Pressure</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Vehicles</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">White 40&deg;</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Up to ~1,500 PSI</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Painted wood, soft siding</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">White 40&deg;</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">1,200-1,500 PSI</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Vinyl siding, fences</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">White 40&deg; or green 25&deg;</td>
