        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="common-mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            8. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using the Red Tip Because It Is in the Set</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A 0 degree tip concentrates the machine's entire output into a jet a few millimetres wide. It removes material rather than dirt, it covers essentially no area so it cannot clean efficiently, and it causes most pressure washer injuries. Store it separately so it cannot be grabbed by mistake.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Expecting Detergent Through a High-Pressure Tip</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The siphon only engages at low pressure, so fitting any high-pressure nozzle stops it drawing entirely. This is the most common reason people believe their soap system has failed. The black tip is the only one that will draw detergent, and no other nozzle substitutes for it.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Skipping Straight to a Narrow Tip</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Starting with green or yellow because the surface looks dirty means finding the material's limit after you have crossed it. Start with white from about ten feet, close in gradually, and step down a single angle only if the wide tip at a reasonable distance genuinely will not shift the dirt.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using a Turbo Nozzle on Wood or Siding</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A turbo tip spins a 0 degree jet in a cone, which gives red-tip force across a wider path. That is excellent on sound concrete and destructive on everything softer. It will fur decking and mark siding just as a red tip would, only faster because it covers more area.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Losing the Black Nozzle</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Every other tip has a workable neighbour, but nothing substitutes for the soap nozzle, so losing it makes the detergent system unavailable entirely. It is also the smallest and least used, which is why it goes missing. Machines with on-board storage solve this; otherwise keep the set in a box.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What do pressure washer nozzle colors mean?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                They follow an industry convention for spray angle. Red is 0 degrees, yellow 15, green 25, white 40, and black is a wide low-pressure soap tip. Wider angles spread the same output over more area, so they hit each square inch less hard and cover more ground per pass.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Which pressure washer nozzle should I use most?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                The white 40 degree tip, for most household work. It suits vehicles, vinyl and painted siding, patio furniture, and rinsing anything after a cleaner has done the chemical work. People under-use it because it feels gentle, but gentle plus a good detergent cleans better than force.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the red 0 degree nozzle for?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Narrow professional tasks like cutting a stubborn deposit off a machine part or clearing a blocked line. On household surfaces it removes material rather than dirt, and because it covers essentially no area it cannot clean efficiently anyway. Most owners never find a legitimate use for it.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Why is no soap coming out of my pressure washer?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Almost always because a high-pressure tip is fitted. The detergent siphon only engages at low pressure, so it stops drawing entirely with any tip other than the black one. Fit the black nozzle, and check the siphon tube and filter are in the detergent and not kinked.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What nozzle should I use on a wood deck?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                The green 25 degree tip at 1,200 to 1,800 PSI, keeping the wand moving constantly and working with the grain. Pausing is what raises the grain and leaves a furred, splintery surface that only sanding will fix. Applying a deck cleaner first lets you use less pressure throughout.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Are turbo nozzles safe?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                On sound concrete, yes, and they are very effective there. Everywhere else, no. A turbo tip spins a 0 degree jet in a cone, so it delivers red-tip force across a wider path and will fur wood and mark siding exactly as a red tip would, only across more area.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Are adjustable nozzles as good as separate tips?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                They are more convenient and usually a little less precise. A variable tip that twists between angles saves swapping nozzles mid-job, which matters on a large surface, but fixed tips generally give a cleaner, more consistent pattern at each angle.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do all pressure washers use the same nozzles?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Most use a standard quarter-inch quick-connect, so tips are interchangeable across brands. Some manufacturers, including certain premium ones, use a proprietary connection instead, which limits you to their accessories permanently. Check the fitting before buying any attachment.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

