        <!-- Closing -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Five colours, one convention across every brand. Red is 0 degrees and belongs in a drawer. Yellow at 15 is a spot tool for stubborn marks on sound hard surfaces. Green at 25 is the general-purpose choice for decks and concrete. White at 40 is the one most household jobs actually want. Black is the soap tip.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            The black nozzle is the one to understand properly, because the detergent siphon only engages at low pressure. Fit any high-pressure tip and no soap will draw, which is why so many owners conclude their detergent system is broken when it is behaving exactly as designed. It is also the tip most often lost, and the only one with no substitute.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Build one habit and the rest follows: start with the widest tip, about ten feet back, on a patch that does not show, and step down a single angle only when the wide tip at a sensible distance genuinely will not shift the dirt. That sequence prevents nearly every case of furred decking, etched siding and stripped paint in this category.
          </p>
        </div>


